# Tailwind spacing pitfall

Also apply when editing `tailwind.config.*` / `next.config.*`.

`tailwind.config.ts` extends `theme.spacing` with **pixel keys** (`10`, `16`, `20`, `24`, `30`, `36`, `40`, `50`, `60`, `70`, `80`, `100`, `120`). Those replace Tailwind’s default rem scale for the same names.

## Broken expectations

| Class | You might expect | Actual here |
|-------|------------------|-------------|
| `w-10` / `h-10` | 2.5rem (40px) | **10px** |
| `p-20` / `gap-20` | 5rem (80px) | **20px** |
| `w-60` | 15rem (240px) | **60px** |
| `mt-40` | 10rem (160px) | **40px** |

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — looks like Tailwind defaults, renders tiny
<div className="w-10 h-10 gap-20" />

// ✅ GOOD — explicit values or safe token names
<div className="w-[40px] h-[40px] gap-5" />
<div className="p-section-y gap-grid-gap" />  // CSS-var tokens from config
```

## Prefer

- Site tokens: `section-y`, `block-y`, `card-pad`, `grid-gap`, `heading-gap`, `inline-gap` (and matching `.section-pad` / utility classes in `globals.css`).
- Arbitrary values (`w-[40px]`, `max-w-[650px]`) when you need a specific size.
- Default scale keys that are **not** overridden (`1`–`8`, `9`, `11`–`14`, `28`, `32`, `48`, `64`, `72`, `96`, fractions, `px`, `full`, …) — or just use arbitrary/`[]` to be safe.

Do not “fix” this by rewriting `theme.spacing` unless the user explicitly asks — it would reshuffle layout site-wide.
